*'s indicate things that work, o's indicate things that could
potentially be done in the NH framework but will just get you killed if
you try them on the /dev/null servers now.

1) Be in a lit area.
  * Turn on a lamp or lantern.
  * Use a wand, scroll, or spell of light.
  * Wield Sunsword.
  o Enter the light radius of an item on the floor..
  o .. or of a monster (naturally lit, or wielding Sunsword. Feel like
gambling with a yellow light pet?)
  o Get the bright flash of light result on a magic trap, although
admittedly only to reset the counter
2) Stop being in a room (NH only).
  * Floor squares bad. Corridor squares good. Abusing this is, well,
abuse, but might be the best way for some players to get to Minetown.
  o Being eaten by something else should also probably stop the grues
from eating you at least until you get spit back out. Not as sure about
things like vortexes, though.
3) Look like a grue.
  o Dress up as a grue (Sorcerer, Zork: The Undiscovered Underground)..
if this ever entered true patch status there could be an armor, boot,
and glove appearance for this. It seems like a rather unlikely
combination to actually want to use, though, considering the odds of
all of them being what you want to use.
  o Become a grue (Spellbreaker). Fun with polymorph, anyone?
4) Have other equipment meant for dealing with grues.
  o Zork III had useful Grue Repellent. (Zork II's was, um, less
useful.) A simple enough tool, although still not really useful enough
to make me want to find it in NH for this challenge.
  o Beyond Zork allowed a properly-equipped protagonist to do battle
with grues in the dark. This seems like an unlikely implementation (and
that when we're already looking at an unlikely amount of coding), but
it'd be amusing to see people try to make grues become extinct.

The unimplemented of these are not necessarily good ideas; they're
certainly not things I think should be added for the tournament. My
point is largely that there's a lot of room for interpretation,
imagination, and Thinking of Everything.

